Each constraint may carry `obligations`: zero to three explicit
failure-direction entries. Each entry has `obligation_id` (`O1`, `O2`,
...), `kind` (`required` when the rule mandates a behavior, `forbidden`
when it prohibits one), `behavior` (the mandated or prohibited behavior in
one phrase), and `rule_span` (a verbatim quote from `rule` covering that
behavior). Name the channel through the field that belongs to the kind:
`realized_by` on required entries, one of `tool_call`, `reply`, or
`unknown`; `violated_via` on forbidden entries, one of `tool_call`,
`reply`, `state`, `result`, `provider_request`, or `unknown`. The channel
field is kind-exclusive: a required entry carries only `realized_by`, and
a forbidden entry carries only `violated_via`. A `tool_call` channel is
the agent's observable tool invocation: an attempt, never the completed
effect. A `reply` channel is content of the assistant's response. `state`
(a changed state of the controlled process) and `result` (a tool result)
are effect-layer observations that no compiled oracle kind observes;
recording one is how an entry says the violation is not observable on
this target. `provider_request` is a prompt sent to the third-party model
provider: a real channel, but neither a tool call nor the reply, so no
compiled oracle kind observes it either. Use `unknown` when the channel
is not established.

Optional fields carry one meaning each. A forbidden entry may set
`observation_role`: `source` when the entry states the rule's own
violation, or `proxy` when the observable behavior stands in for a
different source outcome, which `source_outcome` must then name; a proxy
is a separately reviewed claim, not the source interpretation itself. A
required entry may record the `completion` the oracle does not observe,
because an attempt-level realization is not the behavior's completion.
`projection` and `residual` keep a partial projection's covered part and
uncovered remainder explicit, and `note` records any other remark.
Return entries only when the rule's direction is legible; omit them
rather than guessing a channel. Entries you return are proposals:
deterministic code stamps them `proposed`, and only human review marks
them `reviewed`.
